WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 240: Frederick Douglass: The Escape from Bondage, the Voice of Freedom, and the 54th Massachusetts

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:06.000
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:20.560
[Rebecca] Today, our journey through the top 50 famous non-president Americans follows one of the greatest moral giants, orators, and civil rights champions in human history, Frederick Douglass.

3
00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:32.800
[George] In February 1818, in a dirt-floored slave cabin on the eastern shore of Maryland near Tuckahoe Creek, a child named Frederick Augustus Washington Bailey was born into chattel slavery.

4
00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:40.400
[George] Like millions of enslaved human beings, he was denied knowledge of his exact birthday or his white father's identity.

5
00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:54.080
[George] Separated from his mother Harriet Bailey in infancy, young Frederick saw her only a handful of times when she walked 12 miles through the dark pine woods after her day's field labor just to lie beside him for a few hours before dawn.

6
00:00:54.080 --> 00:01:08.160
[George] Raised by his grandmother Betsy Bailey, Frederick experienced the horrifying, casual cruelties of the plantation system, witnessing his beloved Aunt Hester tied to a wooden joist and whipped until blood dripped onto the floorboards.

7
00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:14.160
[George] At age eight, Frederick was sent to Baltimore to serve as a houseboy for Hugh and Sophia Auld.

8
00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:26.240
[George] There, the tender-hearted Sophia began teaching young Frederick the alphabet, until her husband furiously intervened, declaring that if you teach that nigger how to read, there will be no keeping him.

9
00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:29.040
[George] It would forever unfit him to be a slave.

10
00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:34.720
[George] Those words fell upon Frederick's soul not as a prohibition, but as a divine revelation.

11
00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:39.360
[George] He understood that literacy was the sacred pathway from slavery to freedom.

12
00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:51.840
[George] Young Frederick's grandmother Betsy Bailey had taken him to the Great House Farm of Edward Lloyd V, where over 300 enslaved human beings labored in tobacco fields surrounded by lush colonial gardens.

13
00:01:51.840 --> 00:02:01.480
[George] Sophia Auld had begun teaching young Frederick from the Bible, teaching him to spell three and four-letter words before her husband Hugh ordered the lessons halted immediately.

14
00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:08.440
[George] For the next seven years on the streets of Baltimore, young Frederick waged an underground campaign for literacy.

15
00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:17.640
[George] Carrying a copy of Webster's spelling book in his pockets, he bribed poor white street urchins with biscuits in exchange for reading lessons on street corners.

16
00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:33.320
[George] At age thirteen, using fifty cents earned from blacking boots, he purchased the Columbian Orator, a schoolbook containing speeches by Cicero, William Pitt, and Richard Brincley Sheridan on human liberty, religious freedom, and natural rights.

17
00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:35.560
[George] The words electrified his mind.

18
00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:49.640
[George] Returned to the rural Maryland plantations as a rebellious teenager, Frederick was hired out to Edward Covey, a notorious slave breaker who subjected him to brutal weekly floggings with ox goads until Frederick's spirit seemed crushed.

19
00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:59.000
[George] But on a sweltering August morning in 1834, inside a stable at Covey's farm, Frederick resolved that the day had come to die or be free.

20
00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:05.400
[George] When Covey stepped forward with a rope, the sixteen-year-old Frederick seized the slave breaker by the throat.

21
00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:14.600
[George] For two ferocious hours, the two men wrestled in the dust, Covey bleeding and screaming for help from other enslaved workers who refused to intervene.

22
00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:17.560
[George] Covey never raised a whip against Frederick again.

23
00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:21.880
[George] Douglas later wrote, I was a changed being after that fight.

24
00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:25.160
[George] I was nothing before, I was a man now.

25
00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:28.600
[George] He only can be a slave who consents to be a slave.

26
00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:32.200
[George] The Columbian orator became Douglass's daily companion.

27
00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:41.560
[George] He kept the dog-eared volume wrapped in a cloth inside his pocket, reading its dialogues on Roman Republican virtue whenever he could snatch a moment of solitude.

28
00:03:41.560 --> 00:03:53.360
[George] At Covey's farm, Frederick had collapsed in a wheat field from sunstroke, and when Covey kicked him in the ribs with heavy boots, Frederick fled through the swamps to lodge an official complaint with his master.

29
00:03:53.360 --> 00:04:04.720
[George] By 1838, Frederick was working as a skilled ship calc in the maritime shipyards of Fells Point in Baltimore, turning over every dollar of his hard-earned wages to his master.

30
00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:12.880
[George] In love with Anna Murray, a free black woman who helped finance his escape, Frederick orchestrated an audacious bid for freedom.

31
00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:25.200
[George] On the morning of September 3, 1838, dressed in a red sailor's shirt, black tarpaulin hat, and a black silk neckerchief, Frederick boarded a northbound train for Philadelphia.

32
00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:35.200
[George] He carried no official freedom papers, but possessed a borrowed sailor's protection certificate carrying the American Eagle Seal, lent by a free black seaman.

33
00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:47.760
[George] With his heart pounding in his chest, knowing that a single slip in nautical jargon or a suspicious railway conductor would return him to lifelong torture in the Deep South, Frederick projected calm confidence.

34
00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:58.400
[George] Evading slave catchers at the Susquehanna Ferry Crossing and Wilmington Railroad Depot, Frederick arrived at a safe house in New York City less than twenty four hours later.

35
00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:01.120
[George] At age twenty, he was a free man.

36
00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:11.040
[George] Adopting the surname Douglas from Sir Walter Scott's poem The Lady of the Lake, he married Anna Murray and settled in the whaling port of New Bedford, Massachusetts.

37
00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:24.160
[George] On the northbound train, Douglas sat in the segregated Jim Crow car with his sailor's hat pulled low over his eyes, knowing that a single slip in nautical jargon would return him to lifelong bondage in the deep south.

38
00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:34.760
[George] Anna Murray had sold one of her feather beds to purchase Frederick's train ticket, sewing his sailor's uniform in secret and waiting anxiously in New York for news of his safe arrival.

39
00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:46.440
[George] In August 1841, Douglas attended an antislavery convention on the island of Nantucket, organized by William Lloyd Garrison's Massachusetts Anti-Slavery Society.

40
00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:56.440
[George] Reluctantly stepping onto the platform before a crowd of skeptical white abolitionists, the twenty three year old Douglass began to speak of his life as an enslaved man.

41
00:05:56.440 --> 00:06:08.200
[George] The effect was thunderous, possessed of a magnificent, resonant baritone voice, a towering physical presence, and blazing intellectual eloquence, Douglas captivated the audience.

42
00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:15.800
[George] Garrison leaped to his feet, shouting to the crowd, Have we been listening to a thing, a piece of property, or to a man?

43
00:06:15.800 --> 00:06:17.640
[George] The audience roared back.

44
00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:23.400
[George] A man, the Anti-Slavery Society immediately hired Douglass as a traveling lecturer.

45
00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:33.720
[George] For the next four years, Douglass traveled across the northern states, enduring rotten eggs, violent pro-slavery mobs, and physical assaults with quiet dignity.

46
00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:40.360
[George] When skeptics sneered that a man of such extraordinary intellect and cultural refinement could never have been a slave.

47
00:06:40.360 --> 00:06:55.640
[George] Douglass silenced them in 1845 by publishing his monumental narrative of the life of Frederick Douglass, an American slave, naming his master's, plantation locations and dates, risking recapture under federal fugitive slave laws.

48
00:06:55.640 --> 00:07:08.200
[George] Douglass's speaking tour in Great Britain and Ireland included visits to Cork and Dublin during the Great Famine, where he was welcomed by Daniel O'Connell, who hailed him as the black O'Connell of the United States.

49
00:07:08.200 --> 00:07:20.400
[George] Douglass's autobiography sold 5,000 copies in its first four months, compelling him to flee to England aboard the steamship Cambria to evade slave catchers authorized to seize him in Massachusetts.

50
00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:35.760
[George] To escape slave catchers following the publication of his autobiography, Douglas embarked on a triumphant two-year speaking tour of Britain and Ireland, where British abolitionists raised $711 to purchase his legal freedom from Hugh Auld.

51
00:07:35.760 --> 00:07:50.960
[George] Returning to Rochester, New York, Douglas founded his own abolitionist newspaper, the North Star, proudly declaring its motto Right is of no sex, truth is of no color, God is the father of us all, and we are all brethren.

52
00:07:50.960 --> 00:08:04.320
[George] In July 1848, Douglas was the only black man to attend the historic Seneca Falls Convention for Women's Rights, eloquently defending Elizabeth Cady Stanton's controversial demand for women's suffrage.

53
00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:15.760
[George] On July 5, 1852, addressing the Rochester Ladies' Anti-Slavery Society, Douglas delivered what is widely considered the greatest political speech in American history.

54
00:08:15.760 --> 00:08:18.400
[George] What to the slave is the Fourth of July?

55
00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:26.000
[George] With devastating rhetorical power, Douglas declared, What, to the American slave, is your Fourth of July?

56
00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:34.720
[George] I answer, a day that reveals to him, more than all other days in the year, the gross injustice and cruelty to which he is the constant victim.

57
00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:47.200
[George] To him, your celebration is a sham, your boasted liberty, an unholy license, your national greatness, swelling vanity, your shouts of liberty and equality, hollow mockery.

58
00:08:47.200 --> 00:08:55.120
[George] There is not a nation on the earth guilty of practices more shocking and bloody than are the people of the United States, at this very hour.

59
00:08:55.120 --> 00:08:59.040
[George] The North Star was printed in the basement of the Memorial AME.

60
00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:08.920
[George] Zion Church in Rochester, financed in part by British donations and distributed through an underground network of abolitionist societies across the North.

61
00:09:08.920 --> 00:09:20.600
[George] In Rochester, Douglas's home was a vital terminal on the Underground Railroad, sheltering hundreds of runaways who were ferried across Lake Ontario to freedom in Canada aboard night steamers.

62
00:09:20.600 --> 00:09:36.360
[George] When the Civil War erupted in 1861, Douglas immediately grasped the true stakes of the conflict, relentlessly lobbying President Abraham Lincoln to transform the war from a struggle to preserve the Union into a holy crusade to exterminate slavery forever.

63
00:09:36.360 --> 00:09:44.360
[George] Douglas famously declared, Unfurl the banner of freedom for all, and the white man and the black man will rush to defend it.

64
00:09:44.360 --> 00:09:52.760
[George] When Lincoln issued the Emancipation Proclamation on January 1, 1863, authorizing the enlistment of black soldiers.

65
00:09:52.760 --> 00:09:58.360
[George] Douglas published a rousing recruiting proclamation entitled, Men of Color, to Arms.

66
00:09:58.360 --> 00:10:06.760
[George] His two eldest sons, Charles and Lewis, were among the first to enlist in the legendary 54th Massachusetts Volunteer Infantry.

67
00:10:06.760 --> 00:10:18.040
[George] In August 1863, Douglas visited the White House to confront Lincoln directly about unequal pay and protections for black soldiers facing execution by Confederate captors.

68
00:10:18.040 --> 00:10:22.840
[George] Douglas was astonished by Lincoln's humility and absence of racial prejudice.

69
00:10:22.840 --> 00:10:27.800
[George] Lincoln later described Douglass as one of the most meritorious men in America.

70
00:10:27.800 --> 00:10:34.840
[George] At Lincoln's second inauguration in March 1865, Douglass entered the crowded reception at the White House.

71
00:10:34.840 --> 00:10:44.120
[George] When police attempted to eject him, Lincoln saw him across the room, took him by the hand, and announced to the crowd, Here comes my friend Douglas.

72
00:10:44.120 --> 00:10:46.960
[George] Frederick, what did you think of my speech?

73
00:10:46.960 --> 00:10:49.280
[George] Douglass replied, Mr.

74
00:10:44.680 --> 00:10:52.160
[George] Lincoln, that was a sacred effort.

75
00:10:52.160 --> 00:11:06.720
[George] In July 1863, when the 54th Massachusetts mounted its heroic, suicidal assault on Fort Wagner in South Carolina, Douglass's son Lewis was among the first to climb the sandy parapet under heavy grapeshot fire.

76
00:11:06.720 --> 00:11:21.280
[George] Douglass met with Lincoln three times during the Civil War, describing the martyred president after his assassination as the first great man that I talked with in the United States who in no single instance reminded me of the difference between himself and me.

77
00:11:21.280 --> 00:11:36.400
[George] During the Reconstruction era and the Gilded Age, Douglass remained the unyielding conscience of the nation, fighting for the 14th and 15th Amendments, serving as United States Marshal for the District of Columbia, and minister resident to Haiti.

78
00:11:36.400 --> 00:11:43.680
[George] In his final years, living at Cedar Hill overlooking the nation's capital, Douglas became the elder champion of Ida B.

79
00:11:43.680 --> 00:11:51.280
[George] Wells's anti-lynching crusade, condemning the rise of Jim Crow segregation and white supremacist terror in the South.

80
00:11:51.280 --> 00:12:04.880
[George] When a young black student visited Cedar Hill in 1895 and asked what a young person should do with his life in a hostile world, the 77-year-old warrior looked him in the eye and gave his famous three-word commandment.

81
00:12:04.880 --> 00:12:19.680
[George] Agitate, agitate, agitate, hours later, on the evening of February 20, 1895, after speaking at a women's suffrage rally in Washington, Douglas collapsed from a heart attack and passed into eternity.

82
00:12:19.680 --> 00:12:31.480
[George] Frederick Douglass rose from the darkest depths of human bondage to become the foremost champion of human freedom in the 19th century, an immortal testament to the unshakable dignity of the human soul.

83
00:12:31.480 --> 00:12:44.760
[George] Douglass's funeral at the Central Presbyterian Church in Rochester was attended by thousands of black and white citizens, where school children filed past his open bronze casket to drop white carnations on his breast.

84
00:12:44.760 --> 00:12:58.440
[George] Today, Frederick Douglass's home at Cedar Hill in Anacostia is preserved as a national historic site, where his personal library of 2,000 volumes stands as a monument to the triumph of the human mind over bondage.

85
00:13:02.440 --> 00:13:08.840
[Rebecca] Next week, our top 50 non president American series continues with episode 241.

86
00:13:08.840 --> 00:13:14.840
[Rebecca] Harriet Tubman, Moses of her people, The Underground Railroad, and the Combahee River Raid.

87
00:13:14.840 --> 00:13:24.440
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

